    "record": {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186172101,
        "id": "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595",
        "title": "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "9acb5c34-e84b-564a-89e8-7ab85498538d",
        ":question/problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\u27e8{e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}\u27e9\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\u222c}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={\u222c}_{D}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\u222e}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}(4xy+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}8xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}y\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(2)^{2}y-4(-1)^{2}y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12y\\,dy \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
