  {
    "label": "a377c797-789a-5733-9bba-c0104002ea04",
    "record": {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186172101,
        "id": "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595",
        "title": "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "a377c797-789a-5733-9bba-c0104002ea04",
        ":question/problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\u27e8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2y)-y{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)+2xy\u27e9\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,0),$ and $(0,0).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\u222c}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={\u222c}_{D}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/34/3497b3bbc834879026235f97468f5feb26e91a2b73a4de3da87597bf422ecd33.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2y)-y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\u222e}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}(-2xy+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-{x}^{2}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(1-y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(1-y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-((-1)-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "121bf160-ce9c-579c-9be3-66167c8365bc",
